# Recovery fixtures for misplaced-content handling in tables.
#
# Every expected tree below was derived from the HTML parsing algorithm rather
# than from any html5lib backend:
#
#   * foster parenting, the adoption agency algorithm, active formatting
#     element reconstruction and the foreign-content tree construction rules
#     were applied by hand to each source snippet;
#   * where the official html5lib-tests suite has an equivalent case the
#     front element interactions were compared against its expectation (for
#     example adoption01.dat #10 `<table><a>1<td>2</td>3</table>` and
#     tests1.dat #19 `<b><table><td><i></table>` cover the same shapes);
#   * the resulting trees were cross-checked against a separate browser-grade
#     HTML parser so a misreading of the algorithm would show up before the
#     fixture was committed.
#
# No tree here was copied from the etree, DOM or lxml backend: the point of
# the exercise is that the expectations have an independent source, so that
# "the first backend's output" can never become the answer key.
#
# Sections:
#   #data                the HTML to parse
#   #mode                "document" (default) or "fragment"
#   #document-fragment   container element for fragment cases (default "div")
#   #description         why the case is interesting
#   #document            the expected tree
#
# Format of a tree line: "|", two spaces per nesting level, then one of
#   <name> or <prefix name>          a start tag; a bare name is HTML, and
#                                    html/svg/math/xml/xlink/xmlns prefixes
#                                    select the others
#   prefix name="value"              an attribute of the element above
#   "text"                           a run of text
#   <!-- data -->                    a comment
#   <!DOCTYPE name>                  a doctype

#data
<!doctype html><table class="grid"><b class="fmt">stray<tr><td class="c1">one</td></tr></table>
#mode
document
#description
A formatting element opened directly in the "in table" insertion mode is foster parented: the element and its text move in front of the table while the row content stays inside the cell.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <b>
|       class="fmt"
|       "stray"
|     <table>
|       class="grid"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="c1"
|             "one"

#data
<!doctype html><p><b class="fmt">one<table class="t"><tr><td>two</td></tr></table>three</p>
#mode
document
#description
An unclosed formatting element is closed by the table, then reconstructed after it: "one" stays in the <b> inside <p>, "three" lands in a fresh <b> after the table, and the trailing </p> is adopted inside that reconstructed <b>.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <p>
|       <b>
|         class="fmt"
|         "one"
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             "two"
|     <b>
|       class="fmt"
|       "three"
|       <p>

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><b class="fmt">lead<tr><td class="c">cell</td></tr>trail</b></table>
#mode
document
#description
Foster parenting and formatting-element reconstruction overlap in one snippet: both text runs escape the table and each gets its own reconstructed <b>, in source order.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <b>
|       class="fmt"
|       "lead"
|     <b>
|       class="fmt"
|       "trail"
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="c"
|             "cell"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><tr><td class="c"><b class="a" data-k="1"><p>a</b>b</td></tr></table>
#mode
document
#description
The adoption agency algorithm clones a formatting element that was still open across a <p>: the empty original <b> stays before the paragraph, the clone with the same attributes wraps "a", and "b" is the paragraph's own text.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="c"
|             <b>
|               class="a"
|               data-k="1"
|             <p>
|               <b>
|                 class="a"
|                 data-k="1"
|                 "a"
|               "b"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><tr><td class="c1"><b class="a">one<td class="c2">two</b></table>
#mode
document
#description
A formatting element is open while the parser moves to the next cell; reconstruction does not leak it into the second cell, so "two" is plain text and the second <td> keeps its attribute.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="c1"
|             <b>
|               class="a"
|               "one"
|           <td>
|             class="c2"
|             "two"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="outer"><tr><td class="oc"><b class="fmt"><table class="inner"><tr><td class="ic">x<tr><td class="iy">y</td></tr></table></b></td></tr></table>
#mode
document
#description
Nested tables under an open formatting element: the inner table and both of its rows stay inside the <b> in the outer cell, so neither row is foster parented out.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <table>
|       class="outer"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="oc"
|             <b>
|               class="fmt"
|               <table>
|                 class="inner"
|                 <tbody>
|                   <tr>
|                     <td>
|                       class="ic"
|                       "x"
|                   <tr>
|                     <td>
|                       class="iy"
|                       "y"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><svg class="icon"><circle r="1"/><desc>d</desc></svg><tr><td class="c">cell</td></tr></table>
#mode
document
#description
Foreign content opened in the "in table" mode is foster parented as a whole SVG subtree, and its children stay in the SVG namespace; the following row is still parsed as a row.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <svg svg>
|       class="icon"
|       <svg circle>
|         r="1"
|       <svg desc>
|         "d"
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="c"
|             "cell"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><math class="f"><mi>x</mi></math><tr><td class="c">cell</td></tr></table>
#mode
document
#description
MathML content in the "in table" mode is foster parented too, and its children keep the MathML namespace rather than the HTML one.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <math math>
|       class="f"
|       <math mi>
|         "x"
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="c"
|             "cell"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><tr><td class="c"><svg class="icon"><g>s</g></svg>tail</td></tr></table>
#mode
document
#description
A closed SVG subtree inside a cell is followed by HTML text; the text stays inside the cell and after the SVG element instead of being absorbed into it.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="c"
|             <svg svg>
|               class="icon"
|               <svg g>
|                 "s"
|             "tail"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><tr><td class="c"><svg class="icon" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><use xlink:href="#a"/></svg></td></tr></table>
#mode
document
#description
Namespaced attributes on foreign content inside a table cell survive foster-adjoining-free parsing: xmlns:xlink is an xmlns-namespace attribute and xlink:href is in the XLink namespace, and neither is dropped or moved to an HTML element.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="c"
|             <svg svg>
|               class="icon"
|               xmlns xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"
|               <svg use>
|                 xlink href="#a"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><tr><td class="oc"><svg class="wrapper"><foreignObject><div class="host"><b class="fmt">x</b><table class="inner"><tr><td class="ic">y</td></tr></table></div></foreignObject></svg></td></tr></table>
#mode
document
#description
An HTML integration point inside foreign content establishes a nested table: the <div> and everything below it switch back to the HTML namespace while the <svg>/<foreignObject> wrappers do not.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="oc"
|             <svg svg>
|               class="wrapper"
|               <svg foreignObject>
|                 <div>
|                   class="host"
|                   <b>
|                     class="fmt"
|                     "x"
|                   <table>
|                     class="inner"
|                     <tbody>
|                       <tr>
|                         <td>
|                           class="ic"
|                           "y"

#data
<!doctype html><table class="t"><b class="fmt">lead<tr><td class="oc"><svg class="wrapper"><foreignObject><table class="inner"><tr><td class="ic">in</td></tr></table></foreignObject></svg></td></tr>trail</b></table>
#mode
document
#description
All of the recovery rules at once: the formatting element is foster parented in two separate pieces, and inside the surviving cell a nested table sits under an SVG foreignObject integration point, so HTML and SVG elements alternate in one subtree.
#document
| <!DOCTYPE html>
| <html>
|   <head>
|   <body>
|     <b>
|       class="fmt"
|       "lead"
|     <b>
|       class="fmt"
|       "trail"
|     <table>
|       class="t"
|       <tbody>
|         <tr>
|           <td>
|             class="oc"
|             <svg svg>
|               class="wrapper"
|               <svg foreignObject>
|                 <table>
|                   class="inner"
|                   <tbody>
|                     <tr>
|                       <td>
|                         class="ic"
|                         "in"

#data
<b class="fmt">stray<tr><td class="c">cell</td></tr></b>
#mode
fragment
#document-fragment
table
#description
Fragment parsing with a <table> context: the formatting element and its text move in front of the implied <tbody>, and the row content is built inside it.
#document
| <b>
|   class="fmt"
|   "stray"
| <tbody>
|   <tr>
|     <td>
|       class="c"
|       "cell"

#data
<b class="fmt">stray<tr><td>cell</td></tr></b>
#mode
fragment
#document-fragment
td
#description
Fragment parsing with a <td> context: the stray <tr>/<td> markup is ignored rather than foster parented (there is no table in the stack), so both runs of text end up as adjacent text inside the formatting element.
#document
| <b>
|   class="fmt"
|   "straycell"

#data
<b class="fmt"><table class="inner"><tr><td class="ic">x<tr><td class="iy">y</table></b>
#mode
fragment
#document-fragment
td
#description
A fragment parsed with a <td> context may still contain a nested table; the formatting element wraps it, and both inner rows stay inside the nested table's <tbody>.
#document
| <b>
|   class="fmt"
|   <table>
|     class="inner"
|     <tbody>
|       <tr>
|         <td>
|           class="ic"
|           "x"
|       <tr>
|         <td>
|           class="iy"
|           "y"

#data
<b class="fmt"><table class="inner"><tr><td class="ic">x</td></tr></table></b>
#mode
fragment
#document-fragment
table
#description
With a <table> context and no table on the stack, opening a <b> produces a foster-parented empty formatting element while the nested table still becomes a <tbody> sibling.
#document
| <b>
|   class="fmt"
| <tbody>
|   <tr>
|     <td>
|       class="ic"
|       "x"

#data
<math class="f"><mi>m</mi></math>tail
#mode
fragment
#document-fragment
td
#description
MathML in a table-cell fragment keeps its own namespace, and the trailing HTML text stays outside the MathML subtree.
#document
| <math math>
|   class="f"
|   <math mi>
|     "m"
| "tail"

#data
<svg class="icon"><g>s</g></svg>tail
#mode
fragment
#document-fragment
td
#description
SVG in a table-cell fragment keeps its namespace, and the trailing text stays at the fragment level rather than inside the SVG subtree.
#document
| <svg svg>
|   class="icon"
|   <svg g>
|     "s"
| "tail"

#data
<div class="wrap"><b class="fmt">a</b><table class="t"><tr><td class="c"><i>stay</i></td></tr></table>b</div>
#mode
fragment
#document-fragment
div
#description
Ordinary fragment parsing with no recovery pressure: the table, its cell and the formatting elements keep their nesting and order.
#document
| <div>
|   class="wrap"
|   <b>
|     class="fmt"
|     "a"
|   <table>
|     class="t"
|     <tbody>
|       <tr>
|         <td>
|           class="c"
|           <i>
|             "stay"
|   "b"
